Date: Tue, 21 Dec 93 17:46:07 -0500
From: hhuang@MIT.EDU
Subject: Bayes, Dropouts, and Geniuses

Jeff Fabijanic writes,

>Again, I am not saying "Most dropouts change the world". Most dropouts get
>marginilized and drift. What I am saying is "Many of the people who change
>the world have been dropouts of one sort or another". Quite a different
>statement.

Bayes' theorem illustrates Jeff's point nicely.  Let D be the event
that a person is a dropout, G that a person is a genius (``changes the
world'').  A variant on Bayes' states:

                     P (D | G)     P (D)
                     ---------  =  -----
                     P (G | D)     P (G)

P (D | G) = probability that a genius is a dropout
P (G | D) = probability that a dropout is a genius
P (G), P (D) = probabilities of someone being a genius or dropout

Let r = P (D) / P (G).  Since there are many more dropouts than
geniuses, P (D) is much greater than P (G), hence r is a large number.
So not only is it conceivable that P (D | G) is much greater than
P (G | D), in fact the ratio between these probabilities is precisely
r, the ratio of dropouts to geniuses.

To derive the above, use:

P (B | A) * P (A) = P (B ^ A) = P (A ^ B) = P (A | B) * P (B)

Event A ^ B denotes the conjuction of events A and B.  So for example,
D ^ G is the event that someone is both a dropout and a genius.

