Re the solstice: as far as I know, the Christian celebration *is*,
conceptually at least, on the day of the solstice.  It's just that
in the early days they didn't adjust for the precession of the
equinoxes.  Gregory tried to fix that up, but the trouble was that
the date for Xmas was set in Roman times (when it was called Saturnalia),
several centuries before the date for Easter was fixed.  So Gregory
had the choice of making Xmas occur on the real solstice, or Easter
on the real equinox.  Since officially Xmas is supposed to be on the
birthday of some kid from a Jewish settlement on the West Bank, and
have nothing at all to do with the solstice, (and chances are that
Gregory actually believed that himself - the current Pope seems to),
whereas Easter is officially connected with the equinox, he decided
to skip 10 days rather than 14.
