If I had a reasonable lifespan, so that taking a few years holiday
from adulthood wouldn't be a serious chunk of my real life, of course
I would.  For a similar idea, see Rocheworld (previously the Flight
of the Dragonfly) by Robert L Forward, in which the crew of a
starship take a life-extending but IQ-reducing drug so that they
will live to get to their destination.

I can see this becoming a common - we would check ourselves
into a child-care home for a few years, signing over guardianship
rights over ourselves for the period of our diminished capacity.
Think of the result this would have on the balance of commercial
pressures in the child-care industry.  The centres would *really*
have to compete, instead of the pseudo-competition they engage in
now to attract the parents, since the kids (-to-be) *are* the paying
customers.  The toys and food would be *really*fantastic*, so they
would get our return business.  `I want a peanut-butter-and-tomato-sauce
sandwich *now*, or I won't come back here next time I'm a kid.  And
there aren't enough green crayons.  I want some green crayons.'

And it would revolutionise issues like corporal punishment - do 
