> From: Duncan Frissell <frissell@panix.com>

``My less radical proposal -- carve out free trade zones in Bed/Sty and East 
New York in which no regulations/legislation passed after 1900 apply.  Not 
much welfare but no anti-employment regs either.''

I like that idea, but then again, I liked Snow Crash.

As a person who enjoys the Grateful Dead, I know they *did* have an
effective free trade zone that followed them to many locales.  The fallout
of the WOD caused each location to stamp out the either the activity or
not allow concerts altogether.  The condoned free-enterprise in
Mountain View (Shoreline) rules out a number of saleable items including
food.

Here's a list of places the GD got banned for having such a free trade
zone:  Ventura, Monterey, Stanford, Oakland/Kaiser auditorium.  

So with first hand experience, I'd say that a group of people like 
Dead Heads were extremely enterprising for being a group that seems
very liberal--it was a joy to behold, really.  I remember one show 
at Kaiser auditorium where someone set up a big espresso machine...

Such a free-trade zone would be a great way to get things going in
poor areas.  Given the initial starting conditions, it would not
be immune to armed gangs imposing taxes and regulations, so it 
certainly would need some sort of control hegemony.

Squatter's rights were mentioned earlier (sorry, forgot who), and I'll
add some comments on that:  it would certainly set up a bad precendent
given the situation in Central America.  I was down in Costa Rica
this year and got some perspective on the politics and slippery slope
of this kind of "right".  (And the host of my visit, an American, 
recently had a neighbor machine gunned to death by squatters on the land
he bought.)  They don't come one by one--it's more like 100 at a time
and amazingly they can be very recent immigrants (Nica's, for instance)!

In Costa Rica, there are squatters rights laws on the books and there
are politicians who pander to people looking for a giveaway that keep
these laws working.  The people who own farmland are the most vulnerable
since they have a hard time keeping people off their land.  There are 
no trespassing laws to keep squatters off, but there is a legal recourse
that can take 6 months to evict squatters.  After they are off for a 
specified length of time (it used to be an ineffective 24 hours max, but 
recently it was extended to some longer time), the squatters can come 
back and the whole process starts again.  The effective way to prevent 
squatters is feather bedding: pay people to live on your property.

My friend was concerned that this situation would get out of hand like
it did in neighboring countries--that the government would allow more
and more squatting and then landowners would take up arms and a civil
war would result.  Hopefully that won't happen to Costa Rica.


